// Combined Question Bank — imports from separate files, adds remaining days
import QB1 from './questionBank';
import QB2 from './questionBank2';
import QB3 from './questionBank3';

// Days 11-14 + extras: generate structured Q&A from concepts (15 junior + 15 senior each)
function makeQB(concepts, juniorQs, seniorQs) {
  return { junior: juniorQs, senior: seniorQs };
}

const QB11 = makeQB('Consignment & Free Goods', [
{q:'Explain the four consignment sub-processes and when each applies.',a:'CF (Fill-Up): send stock to customer, no invoice, ownership stays with seller. CI (Issue): customer consumes stock, triggers invoice, ownership transfers. CR (Return): customer returns unused consignment stock. CP (Pickup): seller retrieves remaining consignment stock. Each has its own doc type and item category.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How is consignment stock tracked in SAP?',a:'Special stock indicator W in MM. Tracked per customer in MB52/MMBE. Shows as seller\'s inventory (balance sheet asset) until Consignment Issue. Valued at seller\'s standard cost or MAP.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Why does CF not generate a billing document?',a:'Item category for CF (KBN) has billing relevance = "not relevant." No copy control to billing exists. Structurally prevents billing. Ownership hasn\'t transferred — no revenue event.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What triggers revenue recognition in consignment?',a:'Consignment Issue (CI). This is when: ownership transfers, invoice is generated, COGS is posted, revenue is recognized. CI is the revenue event, not the fill-up.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between Inclusive and Exclusive free goods?',a:'Inclusive: buy 10 pay for 8 (total qty stays 10, 2 are free within). Exclusive: buy 10 get 2 extra (total qty = 12, additional items added). Exclusive increases total quantity.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'When are free goods determined — at order or delivery?',a:'At DELIVERY. Free goods are re-determined at delivery creation, NOT copied from the order. This is a critical interview point. Changes between order and delivery (qty, conditions) affect free goods.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the free goods item category?',a:'TANN — free-of-charge. No billing generated. Delivery-relevant but not billing-relevant. System adds TANN sub-items automatically based on free goods condition records.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Where are free goods condition records maintained?',a:'VBN1 (create), VBN2 (change), VBN3 (display). Access sequence checks material + customer combinations. Records define: minimum qty, free goods qty, inclusive/exclusive flag.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can free goods be different materials from the ordered product?',a:'Yes — cross-material free goods. Condition record specifies "purchase material" (trigger) and "free goods material" (bonus). Different materials allowed. Common in FMCG promotions.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Does Consignment Return (CR) auto-reverse the CI invoice?',a:'No. CR returns goods to consignment stock (W status) but does NOT auto-reverse the CI invoice. A separate credit memo process is needed for financial reversal. Two distinct processes.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does consignment pricing differ from standard pricing?',a:'Can use same or different pricing procedure. Consignment doc types can have different Doc Pricing Procedure in VOV8, leading to different pricing procedures. Or same procedure with consignment-specific condition types.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is free goods scaling?',a:'Quantity breakpoints with different free goods ratios per tier. Buy 10 get 1, buy 20 get 3, buy 50 get 10. Maintained as scales on the free goods condition record, similar to pricing scales.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How is consignment stock valued?',a:'At seller\'s standard cost or moving average price until CI. It\'s the seller\'s inventory (balance sheet asset). At CI: cost transfers to COGS. Valuation unchanged while in consignment status.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can partial Consignment Issue be done?',a:'Yes. If customer has 100 consignment units, they can issue 50. Remaining 50 stays as consignment (W). Each CI can be any quantity up to available consignment balance.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How do free goods interact with intercompany billing?',a:'Free goods (TANN) are typically NOT included in IC billing. IC transfer pricing applies only to revenue-generating items. Free goods cost is absorbed by the selling entity as a marketing/promotion expense.',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'Client wants consignment with automatic CI when stock is consumed in customer\'s system. Integration approach?',a:'Customer system sends consumption data via EDI (DESADV or custom IDoc). SAP receives consumption qty → auto-creates CI document → triggers invoice. Integration via PI/PO middleware. Key: reconciliation process to match physical consumption with SAP consignment balance. Periodic physical count for alignment.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Free goods condition record exists but delivery shows no free goods. Systematic diagnosis?',a:'(1) Free goods are RE-DETERMINED at delivery, not copied from order. (2) Check: is free goods determination active at delivery level in config? (3) Is the condition record valid for the delivery date (not order date)? (4) Does the delivery qty meet the minimum threshold? (5) Is the item category on the delivery item compatible with free goods sub-items? (6) Check access sequence — does the field combination match?',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design a consignment process with quarterly reconciliation and automatic CI for discrepancies.',a:'Monthly: seller sends consignment stock report to customer. Quarterly: physical count at customer site vs SAP consignment balance. Discrepancy: auto-CI for used/missing units (invoice customer), auto-CR for damaged goods (return to seller). Reconciliation report: MB52 consignment balance vs customer-reported stock. Process documented in agreement.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client has free goods across 500 material-customer combinations. Maintenance burden is high. Simplification?',a:'(1) Use higher-level access: Material Group + Customer Group instead of individual material + customer. Fewer records, broader coverage. (2) Use condition supplements: one trigger condition adds multiple free goods automatically. (3) Standardize free goods ratios per product category. (4) Consider: is the complexity justified by business value? Simplify the promotional structure itself.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Consignment stock at customer is expiring. Who bears the cost and how does SAP handle it?',a:'Seller bears the cost — it\'s their inventory. Options: (1) CP (pickup) before expiry — seller retrieves and scraps. (2) CI at reduced price — customer takes ownership at markdown. (3) Write-off via MM movement type for expired consignment. Consignment agreements should define expiry handling and cost responsibility.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Free goods creating margin erosion — business doesn\'t realize the cost. How to make it visible?',a:'(1) Statistical condition type on free goods items showing the "cost" of the free item. (2) CO-PA analysis: track free goods cost as a separate characteristic. (3) Report: monthly free goods value by customer/material group. (4) Free goods budget control: condition record limits per customer per period. Make the invisible cost visible in reporting.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design error handling when free goods material is out of stock at delivery.',a:'(1) If free goods material unavailable: ATP blocks the free goods sub-item. (2) Options: deliver without free goods (remove TANN item), substitute with alternative free material (material determination), or delay delivery until free goods available. (3) Config: free goods requirement routine can check availability and suppress if unavailable. (4) Business decision: is the sale more important than the promotion?',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Consignment Fill-Up for 1000 units but customer only has space for 500. Partial CF?',a:'Yes — partial CF is standard. Deliver 500 via CF. Remaining 500 stays on the order as open schedule line. Next CF delivery when customer has space. Consignment balance at customer: 500 units (W stock).',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants to charge consignment stock holding fees after 90 days. SAP approach?',a:'Not standard SAP functionality. Custom approach: (1) Track consignment fill-up dates per batch/lot. (2) Scheduled job checks age of consignment stock (delivery date + 90 days). (3) Auto-create debit memo or special invoice for holding fees. (4) Alternative: include holding fee clause in consignment agreement with periodic manual billing.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Cross-material free goods: buy Product A (printer), get Product B (toner) free. But toner has batches. How does batch determination work for free goods?',a:'Free goods material (toner) goes through standard batch determination at delivery — same as any other material. FIFO/FEFO batch strategy applies. The free goods item is treated as a normal delivery item for batch purposes, just with TANN item category (no billing).',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Multiple free goods conditions overlap for the same customer-material. Which wins?',a:'Depends on access sequence hierarchy and condition exclusion rules. If both are valid: system may apply both (stacking) or use exclusion to select the best one. Check: free goods exclusion group settings. Best practice: avoid overlapping conditions — design clean, non-overlapping promotional structures.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Consignment and intercompany: entity A has consignment at customer, entity B supplies to A. How many billing streams?',a:'Three streams: (1) IC invoice B→A (transfer pricing for stock supplied). (2) No billing at CF (A→customer). (3) Invoice at CI (A→customer). Entity A\'s margin = CI price minus IC cost. Consignment stock is A\'s asset, supplied by B.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client reports consignment balance in SAP doesn\'t match physical count at customer. Investigation?',a:'(1) Check all CF/CI/CR/CP documents for the customer — any missing postings? (2) Check: was any stock scrapped, damaged, or used without CI? (3) Check: goods movements outside SD (MM direct movements on consignment stock). (4) Reconciliation: MB52 balance vs customer-reported. (5) Adjust via inventory adjustment (MI07) with justification. Root cause: missing CI postings most common.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Free goods at delivery: customer ordered 100, free goods gives 10 extra. But truck has space for only 100. What happens?',a:'Delivery qty for paid items = 100, free goods = 10 TANN. Total picking qty = 110. If truck capacity is 100: options (1) reduce paid qty to 90 + 10 free = 100. (2) Remove free goods this delivery (deliver 100 paid, next delivery includes free goods). (3) Split into two deliveries. Business decision based on customer relationship and logistics cost.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design a free goods approval process for high-value free goods exceeding ₹50K.',a:'(1) Requirement routine on free goods condition type: if free goods value > ₹50K, set delivery block. (2) Workflow notification to sales manager. (3) Manager reviews free goods justification, approves/rejects. (4) Approval removes delivery block. (5) Without approval: free goods items blocked on delivery. (6) Reporting: monthly free goods value by approver for audit.',type:'scenario'},
]);

const QB12 = makeQB('Third Party & IPO', [
{q:'What is the third-party sales process?',a:'Seller A receives order from customer C. Instead of delivering, A creates PO to supplier B. Supplier B delivers directly to customer C. Seller A invoices customer C. No outbound delivery from A.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the Item Category Group for third-party?',a:'BANS on the material master. Triggers item category TAS on the order, which creates a Purchase Requisition instead of a delivery requirement.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the document flow for third-party?',a:'Sales Order → auto Purchase Requisition → Purchase Order (ME21N) → statistical Goods Receipt (MIGO) → Supplier Invoice (MIRO) → Customer Billing (VF01). No outbound delivery from seller.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a statistical GR in third-party?',a:'Goods don\'t physically arrive at seller\'s warehouse. GR is for tracking and cost matching only. Goods go from supplier directly to customer. "Statistical" = no physical inventory at seller.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does pricing work in third-party?',a:'Two independent pricing streams: SD pricing (seller\'s price to customer) and MM pricing (supplier\'s price to seller). Seller\'s margin = customer price minus supplier cost. Completely independent.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can seller add markup on third-party items?',a:'Yes. SD pricing is independent of MM pricing. Seller sets any price regardless of supplier cost. Margin management is the core business value of third-party selling.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between third-party and drop-shipping?',a:'Same process, different terminology. "Drop-ship" is the business term. "Third-party processing" is the SAP configuration term. Functionally identical.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can stocked and third-party items coexist on one order?',a:'Yes. Each line item independently follows its item category. TAN lines create delivery requirements. TAS lines create purchase requisitions. Mixed order, different processing per line.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Individual Purchase Order (IPO)?',a:'Variant of third-party where seller creates PO, receives goods at their warehouse, inspects/adds value, then delivers to customer. Goods touch seller\'s warehouse. More control than pure third-party.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'When can the seller create the customer invoice in third-party?',a:'Typically after statistical GR is posted (confirms supplier shipped). Configuration can allow billing at PO creation (advance) or at GR posting. Copying requirement in VTFA controls timing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How do you convert a third-party material to stocked?',a:'Change Item Category Group from BANS to NORM on the material master. Next orders get TAN instead of TAS. Existing open orders stay as TAS. No new material needed — just master data change.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the supplier\'s role in third-party tracking?',a:'Supplier ships directly to customer. Communicates shipment status via ASN (Advance Shipping Notification) via EDI. Statistical GR in seller\'s system confirms delivery. Seller tracks via PO status.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How are returns handled in third-party?',a:'Returns order in SD creates credit memo for customer. Separately: return PO/debit note to supplier. Logistics: goods may go back to supplier directly or via seller. Two independent return streams.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens if supplier ships wrong quantity?',a:'Statistical GR for actual qty shipped. SD billing qty limited to GR qty (copying requirement checks). Remaining qty stays open on order. Seller follows up with supplier for balance or cancels.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does the PR get created from the sales order?',a:'Schedule line category for TAS has procurement indicators that trigger automatic PR creation at order entry. PR appears in MM, referencing the SO. Procurement team converts PR to PO (ME21N).',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'Client has mix of stocked and third-party for the same material — sometimes from stock, sometimes drop-shipped based on availability. Design?',a:'Material with NORM as default. When stock unavailable: manual change to third-party item category at order entry (user decision). Or: automatic via availability check — if ATP fails, system proposes third-party (custom enhancement). Better: two materials — stocked version and third-party version. Cleanest: let user decide at order entry with clear item category override.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Third-party PR/PO creation is slow for orders with 500+ line items. Root cause and optimization?',a:'PR creation is synchronous per line item in standard. Each triggers procurement checks. For high volume: (1) background PR creation job, (2) batch processing, (3) MRP optimization for direct PR without planning run, (4) SD enhancement for async PR processing. Also: check if all 500 lines truly need individual PRs or can be consolidated.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Supplier raises price after PO is created but before delivery. Impact on seller?',a:'SD customer price unchanged — independent pricing. Only MM cost side affected. Seller\'s margin decreases. Options: (1) negotiate with supplier, (2) absorb margin hit, (3) create change PO with updated price. Customer sees no change. Update future condition records if the price increase is permanent.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design a third-party quality check process where seller wants to inspect a sample before supplier ships the full order.',a:'Not pure third-party. Hybrid: (1) PO to supplier for sample qty (sent to seller for inspection). (2) If approved: PO amendment for full qty with direct delivery to customer. (3) If rejected: cancel PO, find alternative supplier. Alternatively: supplier sends sample independently, main PO for direct delivery contingent on sample approval.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'IC third-party: selling entity A, sister entity B acts as supplier, delivers to external customer. How many documents and pricing streams?',a:'Documents: SO at A, IC PO from A to B, delivery from B to customer, external invoice (A→customer), IC invoice (B→A). Three pricing streams: customer price (SD at A), IC transfer price (IC between A and B), and B\'s internal cost. Two billing documents: external and IC.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Third-party returns: customer wants refund, supplier won\'t accept returns. Who bears the cost?',a:'Seller bears the cost. SD: credit memo to customer (seller\'s obligation). MM: no return PO possible if supplier won\'t accept. Seller absorbs the returned goods (if they come back) or writes off the cost. Commercial risk of third-party selling.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants serial number tracking on third-party items. Supplier assigns serials. How to track?',a:'Supplier communicates serial numbers via ASN (EDI IDoc). Statistical GR records serial numbers. Document flow links: serial → GR → PO → SO → customer. Seller has traceability without physical handling. Warranty tracking possible via serial number.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design a hybrid process: seller has 80% of order from stock, remaining 20% drop-shipped from supplier. One order, one invoice to customer.',a:'Two line items (or split of one): 80% with item cat TAN (standard delivery from stock), 20% with item cat TAS (third-party PR). TAN items: normal delivery + PGI. TAS items: PO + statistical GR. Billing: both items on one invoice (collective billing or both on same billing due list). Customer sees one invoice with all items.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Performance issue: 200 third-party orders per day, each creating a PR that procurement must manually convert to PO. Automation?',a:'(1) Automatic PO creation from PR: schedule line category with "automatic PO" indicator creates PO directly, skipping PR step. (2) If PR step needed for approval: workflow auto-approves PRs below threshold, procurement manually reviews only high-value. (3) Source list and purchasing info record auto-assign vendor. (4) ME59N: mass PR→PO conversion batch job.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Third-party: supplier goes bankrupt mid-order. 50% shipped, 50% pending. Recovery process?',a:'(1) For shipped 50%: statistical GR posted, invoice customer normally. (2) For pending 50%: cancel remaining PO, find alternative supplier, create new PO. (3) If new supplier: potentially different cost (margin impact). (4) Customer communication: delay notification for remaining 50%. (5) Financial: write off any advance payments to bankrupt supplier.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Explain the accounting entries in a complete third-party cycle.',a:'At statistical GR: no inventory entry (statistical only). At supplier invoice (MIRO): Debit cost/expense, Credit vendor payable. At customer billing (VF01): Debit customer receivable, Credit revenue. Seller\'s profit = customer revenue minus supplier cost. No inventory movement at seller\'s books.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Client wants to track third-party delivery status in real-time for customer service agents.',a:'(1) Document flow on SO shows PO status including GR. (2) ASN via EDI provides tracking details. (3) Custom cockpit/report: SO → PO → GR status summary per customer. (4) PO delivery monitoring (ME2M) with follow-up flags. (5) Integration with supplier\'s tracking portal via API/BTP for real-time status. Customer service checks one screen for all delivery statuses.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Third-party with consignment: supplier sends goods on consignment to customer via seller\'s PO. Feasible?',a:'Non-standard combination. Would need: PO for consignment from supplier (consignment info record), statistical receipt as consignment at customer, CI process when customer uses. Complex: combines MM consignment (supplier to seller) with SD third-party (direct to customer). Likely needs custom development. Consider simplifying the process design.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client asks whether third-party can work with blanket POs to the supplier for better pricing.',a:'Yes — outline agreement (contract) with supplier in MM. Individual SOs trigger PO releases against the contract. Supplier honors contract price for all releases. PR from SO references the contract. Better pricing through committed volume without separate PO negotiation per order.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design reconciliation process between SD revenue and MM cost for third-party transactions.',a:'Monthly: (1) Report all third-party SOs with billing status and amount. (2) Report corresponding POs with MIRO (supplier invoice) status and amount. (3) Match: each SO line → PO line → billing → supplier invoice. (4) Unmatched: missing billings (revenue gap) or missing supplier invoices (cost gap). (5) Margin analysis: revenue minus cost per transaction. (6) Alerts for margin below threshold.',type:'scenario'},
]);

const QB13 = makeQB('STO & Intercompany', [
{q:'What is a Stock Transport Order (STO)?',a:'Transfers stock between plants. Same Company Code = pure logistics (no billing). Different Company Codes = intercompany, requires IC billing for transfer pricing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is movement type 301?',a:'One-step stock transfer. Stock moves immediately from plant A to plant B. No in-transit tracking. Simple and fast for same-location or nearby plants.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is two-step STO (303/305)?',a:'303: goods issue from plant A (stock goes to "in-transit"). 305: goods receipt at plant B (stock arrives). In-transit period allows tracking goods in movement. More realistic for distant plants.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'When is IC billing required for STO?',a:'When plants are in DIFFERENT Company Codes. Different CCs = different legal entities. IC billing needed for transfer pricing, entity-level accounting, and legal compliance.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are the two invoices in intercompany sales?',a:'(1) External invoice: selling entity → customer (revenue). (2) IC invoice: supplying entity → selling entity (transfer cost). Two independent billing streams.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is an IC customer?',a:'Internal representation of the other SalesOrg/CC. Entity A has an IC customer master representing Entity B. Used for IC billing and pricing determination.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How is IC customer modeled in S/4HANA?',a:'One Business Partner with both customer role (FLCU00/01) and vendor role (FLVN00/01). CVI syncs both sides. Single entity for IC partner management.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is transfer pricing?',a:'Internal price between legal entities. Determines how revenue is split between supplying and selling entities. Must be arms-length per OECD guidelines. Methods: cost-plus, market-based, negotiated.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What triggers IC billing?',a:'The supplying entity creates the IC invoice after goods issue (PGI) on the delivery. Can be manual or automated via scheduled billing run (VF06).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is in-transit stock?',a:'Stock that has left plant A (303 movement) but not yet received at plant B (305 pending). Tracked in MM as stock in transit. Visible in MMBE/MB52.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the IC pricing procedure?',a:'Separate pricing procedure for IC billing. Assigned via IC billing type in VOV8. Contains IC-specific condition types with own access sequences. Independent from customer-facing pricing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does STO differ from a regular purchase order?',a:'STO transfers stock between plants within the same company (or between companies in the same SAP system). Regular PO procures from external vendor. STO uses delivery; PO uses goods receipt.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is IC reconciliation?',a:'Month-end check: IC revenue at entity A must equal IC cost at entity B. Differences investigated: timing, FX, manual overrides, incomplete billing. Must resolve before group consolidation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the most common cause of IC reconciliation differences?',a:'Timing. Goods issued in period N (December) but IC billing runs in period N+1 (January). Revenue and cost posted in different periods. Fix: align IC billing timing with delivery timing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can STO cross country borders?',a:'Yes, but adds complexity: customs duties, import/export documentation, VAT/GST treatment, withholding tax, and transfer pricing documentation requirements. Tax/trade compliance team must be involved.',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'Customer orders from CC1, stock only available at CC3 plant. Walk through the complete document and billing flow.',a:'SO at CC1 SalesOrg → delivery from CC3 plant → PGI at CC3 → External invoice CC1→Customer (revenue for CC1) → IC invoice CC3→CC1 (transfer price, revenue for CC3, cost for CC1). Two billing docs, two GL posting sets. CC1 margin = customer price minus IC transfer price. CC3 margin = IC transfer price minus production cost.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'IC transfer price is ₹1200 but customer price is ₹1000. Is this valid?',a:'System allows it, but selling entity (CC1) takes a LOSS (buys at 1200, sells at 1000). Indicates: transfer pricing policy misaligned, customer price too low, or IC condition records wrong. Tax authorities may also question loss-making intercompany transactions. Review transfer pricing policy.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'STO shipped but IC invoice not created. Month-end impact?',a:'Stock physically at receiving plant but no IC cost posted. Receiving entity\'s inventory is understated. Supplying entity\'s IC revenue is understated. Financial statements misstate ownership. IC billing must be timely. Common audit finding.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design automated IC billing with zero manual intervention.',a:'(1) Output type on IC delivery triggers IC billing at GI. (2) Or: VF06 scheduled background job for IC doc types only. (3) Copying requirement ensures GI exists before IC billing. (4) Auto-output on IC invoice (email/print). (5) Monitoring: daily check for unprocessed IC deliveries. Zero manual intervention end-to-end.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'50 IC relationships across 10 CCs. Reconciliation takes 2 weeks monthly. How to optimize?',a:'(1) Automate IC billing (same-day as GI). (2) Standardize IC pricing to reduce variance sources. (3) Implement SAP ICMR (IC Matching and Reconciliation) for automated comparison. (4) Same-currency IC where possible (reduces FX differences). (5) Zero-override policy on IC pricing (exceptions require approval). (6) Target: 2-day reconciliation.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Multi-level IC: CC1 sells to customer, orders from CC2, CC2 sources from CC3. Complete billing flow?',a:'CC3→CC2: IC invoice 1 (CC3\'s production cost + markup). CC2→CC1: IC invoice 2 (CC2\'s cost + markup). CC1→Customer: external invoice (customer price). Three billing documents. CC3 margin on IC1, CC2 margin on IC2-IC1, CC1 margin on customer price-IC2. Three-way reconciliation at consolidation.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'OECD audit: tax authority challenges IC prices. What SAP data supports defense?',a:'IC condition records showing pricing methodology. CO-PA margin analysis per entity (comparable profitability). IC transaction volume reports. Pricing procedure documentation. Combined with external benchmarking studies (comparable uncontrolled price method or transactional net margin method). SAP data provides the factual basis; external studies provide the arms-length validation.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'S/4HANA Central Finance impact on IC processing?',a:'Central Finance replicates financial documents from multiple ERPs into central S/4HANA. IC matching and elimination done centrally. Simplifies multi-system IC reconciliation. Enables group-level real-time IC visibility. Long-term strategy for complex multi-system IC landscapes.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Client has seasonal business. IC billing spike in December causes processing delays. Solution?',a:'(1) Pre-schedule IC billing jobs with higher frequency in peak months. (2) Parallel processing: split IC billing by SalesOrg or plant. (3) Consider accrual-based IC: post estimated IC cost at GI, true-up when IC invoice processes. (4) Performance tuning on billing tables. (5) Alert monitoring for IC billing backlog.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Cross-border STO between India and UAE entities. Tax and compliance considerations?',a:'India: export documentation, customs invoice, shipping bill, GST under LUT/bond. UAE: import duties, customs clearance. Transfer pricing: documented per Indian and UAE regulations. Withholding tax may apply. Permanent establishment risk assessment. Currency: INR vs AED exchange rate management. Transfer pricing report for both jurisdictions.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'IC customer and IC vendor master data — what happens if they\'re out of sync?',a:'IC billing fails or posts to wrong accounts. IC customer drives billing/pricing side. IC vendor drives procurement/cost side. In S/4HANA: BP syncs both via CVI, reducing sync risk. In ECC: manual maintenance required on both sides. Periodic audit: compare IC customer and IC vendor master data for consistency.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design IC pricing that auto-derives from material standard cost plus 8% markup, with no manual condition records.',a:'IC condition type configured with formula-based calculation: reference material standard cost from accounting view + 8% via pricing procedure calculation step. Formula routine in VOFM reads MBEW (material valuation) table. No manual condition records needed — price auto-calculates from master data. Update when standard cost changes (annual cost roll).',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants visibility of IC profit/loss per product line across all entities.',a:'CO-PA (Profitability Analysis) with IC elimination. Configure: external revenue conditions AND IC cost conditions captured per product line. IC profit = external revenue minus IC transfer cost minus direct costs. CO-PA reporting with product line as characteristic. Consolidation view: eliminate IC to show true group margin.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Permanent establishment (PE) risk — why should an SD consultant care?',a:'If SD transactions show a SalesOrg routinely acting as agent for a foreign entity (taking orders, negotiating terms), the foreign entity\'s country may claim PE — creating tax obligations. SD document flow, SalesOrg assignments, and IC agreements are PE evidence. SD consultant must design org structure to avoid unintended PE patterns. Tax counsel must review IC design.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'STO with batch management — how does batch transfer work?',a:'At GI from plant A: specific batch consumed (batch determination or manual). At GR at plant B: same batch number can be maintained (batch across plants) or new batch created (plant-specific batches). Config: batch level (client vs plant) determines whether batch numbers carry across plants. Most common: plant-level batches = new batch at receiving plant.',type:'conceptual'},
]);

const QB14 = makeQB('IC Billing & Advanced', [
{q:'What is the IC billing document type?',a:'IV — intercompany invoice. Created by supplying entity. Posts IC revenue at supplying CC and IC cost at receiving CC. Separate from external billing (F2). Own number range, pricing procedure, output type.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does IC account determination differ from external?',a:'VKOA entries for IC use separate account keys and GL accounts. IC Revenue GL (e.g., 4900) at supplier. IC Cost GL (e.g., 5900) at receiver. These must net to zero in group consolidation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are the transfer pricing methods?',a:'Cost-plus: supplier cost + markup %. Market-based: arms-length market price. Negotiated: agreed internal price. Method maintained via IC condition type and access sequence. Must comply with OECD guidelines.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is IC reconciliation?',a:'Month-end process: IC revenue at entity A must equal IC cost at entity B. Differences arise from timing, FX, manual overrides, incomplete billing. Must resolve before group financial consolidation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can one customer order have items from multiple Company Codes?',a:'Yes. Different line items can ship from different plants/CCs. System creates IC invoice per CC boundary plus external invoice to customer for all items. Complex but standard SAP.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Why must IC number ranges be separate from external?',a:'Easy identification of IC vs external documents. Reporting segregation. Clean audit trail. Consolidation elimination matching. Prevents confusion between internal and external billing.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the IC pricing procedure assigned via?',a:'IC billing type in VOV8 → Document Pricing Procedure → IC Pricing Procedure. Independent from external pricing. Contains IC-specific condition types.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What exchange rate applies to IC billing?',a:'IC billing date exchange rate — when the IC invoice is created and GL entries posted. Rate type configurable: average, spot, or month-end rate. Differences vs GI date rate cause reconciliation gaps.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is ICMR?',a:'Intercompany Matching and Reconciliation. SAP tool for automated comparison of IC transactions across entities. Identifies mismatches, proposes adjustments. Replaces manual reconciliation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does group consolidation eliminate IC?',a:'IC revenue at entity A and IC cost at entity B should be equal and opposite. In consolidation: both are eliminated (netted to zero). Only external revenue and external costs remain in group financials.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a multi-currency IC challenge?',a:'Entity A operates in EUR, Entity B in USD. IC invoice: exchange rate at billing date determines amounts. If rate changes between GI and billing: EUR/USD amounts differ on each side. Requires FX revaluation for reconciliation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the complete GL posting for IC billing?',a:'At supplying CC: Debit IC Receivable, Credit IC Revenue. At receiving CC: Debit IC Cost/Procurement, Credit IC Payable. Four GL entries total across two company codes.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How do you automate IC billing?',a:'(1) Auto-billing flag on IC billing type. (2) Scheduled VF06 job for IC doc types. (3) Output trigger at GI posts. (4) Copying requirement ensures GI exists. (5) Auto-output (print/email) on IC invoice.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the IC billing timing gap risk?',a:'If goods issued in December but IC billing runs in January: inventory at receiving plant but no IC cost posted. Financial statements misstate ownership and profitability for December. Both entities affected.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What documentation does OECD require for transfer pricing?',a:'Master file (group overview, TP policies), Local file (entity-specific TP documentation, benchmarking), Country-by-Country Report (CbCR for revenue, profit, tax per jurisdiction). SAP data supports local file with transaction details.',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'IC revenue ₹1.2M at entity A, IC cost ₹1.18M at entity B. ₹20K difference. Complete investigation.',a:'(1) Extract all IC transactions for the period line by line. (2) Check: any deliveries without IC invoice (billing completeness)? (3) Exchange rate check: what rate used on each transaction? Different billing dates = different rates. (4) Manual overrides on any IC condition types? (5) Rounding: different precision at each entity. (6) Tax adjustments: withholding tax deducted at one side? Root cause determines fix: operational (run missing billing), FX (revaluation entry), or manual (adjust with documentation).',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Design IC billing automation for a company with 8 entities and 28 IC relationships.',a:'(1) Standardize all IC doc types, pricing procedures, and condition types across entities. (2) Single scheduled VF06 job runs every evening after GI cutoff. (3) Selection: IC billing types only. (4) Auto-output: IC invoice emailed to receiving entity controller. (5) Daily reconciliation report: compares IC billing count vs IC delivery count. (6) Exception alert: any delivery >24 hours without IC invoice. (7) Monthly: ICMR tool for full reconciliation with automated matching.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants IC pricing to change quarterly based on updated standard costs. Process design?',a:'(1) Quarterly cost roll updates material standard cost (CK40N). (2) IC condition type uses formula referencing standard cost + markup. (3) Condition records auto-adjust when standard cost changes (if formula-based) OR condition records updated quarterly via batch update. (4) Document: quarterly transfer pricing update memo for compliance. (5) Reconciliation: Q1 deliveries billed at Q1 costs, Q2 at Q2 costs — no cross-quarter mixing.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Multi-currency IC revaluation at month-end. Entity A (INR) bills Entity B (USD). Walk through the FX treatment.',a:'At IC billing: INR amount converted to USD at billing date rate. If GI was on Dec 15 (rate 83.5) and IC billing on Dec 28 (rate 84.0): amounts differ. At month-end: unrealized FX gain/loss on IC receivable/payable revaluation (FAGL_FC_VAL or F.05). At settlement: realized FX gain/loss when payment clears. Three rate dates: GI, billing, settlement.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Group consolidation: IC eliminations show ₹50K residual that won\'t net to zero. Systematic resolution.',a:'(1) Run ICMR: match every IC transaction A-side vs B-side. (2) Unmatched transactions: missing billing on one side (operational fix). (3) Amount mismatches: FX differences (revaluation entry), rounding (adjustment entry). (4) Timing: cross-period transactions (accrual at the short side). (5) Residual after all fixes: manual consolidation adjustment with audit documentation. Target: zero residual through operational discipline.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'Tax authority in Country A demands detailed transfer pricing documentation for 500 IC transactions. What SAP reports support this?',a:'(1) IC billing document list with amounts, dates, materials (VF05 filtered for IC). (2) IC condition records showing pricing methodology (VK13 for IC condition types). (3) CO-PA: profitability per entity per product (demonstrates arms-length margins). (4) Material standard cost history (CKM3) showing cost basis. (5) IC reconciliation report showing matched transactions. Package as appendices to the Local File documentation.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client has two-tier ERP: HQ on S/4HANA Private Cloud, 5 subsidiaries on S/4HANA Public Cloud. IC design?',a:'No native IC billing across two separate S/4HANA systems. Integration via API: (1) HQ system creates IC PO to subsidiary via API. (2) Subsidiary creates delivery and IC invoice via API. (3) IC matching via Central Finance or custom reconciliation service on BTP. (4) Master data synchronization: BP, material, pricing via MDG or integration middleware. Complexity significantly higher than single-system IC.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants to implement SAP ICMR. What does it replace and what are the prerequisites?',a:'Replaces: manual Excel-based reconciliation. Prerequisites: (1) Consistent IC document types across entities, (2) IC partner identification on all documents, (3) Matching rules defined (by document reference, amount tolerance, date tolerance), (4) Both sides of IC transaction must be in the same SAP system (or replicated via Central Finance). ICMR automates matching, highlights exceptions, tracks resolution.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'IC billing and revenue recognition under IFRS 15 — any interaction?',a:'IC transactions are typically excluded from IFRS 15 (they\'re eliminated in consolidation). However: if the IC arrangement constitutes a separate performance obligation (e.g., IC services), IFRS 15 may apply to the IC contract. For standard goods transfer: IC billing is at transfer pricing, not at fair value, so IFRS 15 allocation is not relevant. Confirm with finance/audit team.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Design a monthly IC close process from Day 1 to Day 5 after month-end.',a:'Day 1: Billing completeness check — run IC billing for any remaining unprocessed deliveries. Day 2: ICMR matching run — automated comparison of IC transactions across entities. Day 3: Exception resolution — investigate and resolve unmatched items (missing billing, FX differences). Day 4: FX revaluation on IC receivables/payables. Day 5: IC reconciliation sign-off by controllers at both entities. Target: zero unresolved IC differences by Day 5.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client asks: "Why can\'t we just use one Company Code for everything and avoid IC completely?" Pros and cons?',a:'Pros: zero IC complexity, no transfer pricing, no reconciliation, simpler month-end. Cons: one legal entity for everything — doesn\'t work for multi-country operations (each country needs its own legal entity for tax, regulatory, employment law). Even within one country: subsidiary structures for liability isolation, tax benefits, M&A flexibility. IC complexity is the price of legal structure flexibility.',type:'conceptual'},
]);

const QBOther = makeQB('Other Processes', [
{q:'What is the returns document flow?',a:'Returns Order (RE) → Return Delivery (goods receipt into returns storage location) → Credit Memo (billing reversal). Document flow links all three. Returns do NOT auto-reverse the original invoice.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between CR and RE?',a:'CR (Credit Memo Request) = financial only, no goods movement. RE (Returns Order) = physical goods return with delivery + credit. Different processes for different business scenarios.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Material Determination?',a:'Auto-substitution of one material for another when ordered material is unavailable. Uses condition records (VB11). Can be mandatory (automatic) or optional (user prompted).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Listing vs Exclusion?',a:'Listing = whitelist (only listed materials can be sold to customer). Exclusion = blacklist (specific materials blocked). Both checked at order entry via condition records (VB01).',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Down Payment?',a:'Partial payment before delivery. Posted as special GL (not revenue). At final invoice: down payment cleared against invoice amount. Balance = remaining payment due.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What pricing does a returns credit memo use?',a:'Original order/invoice pricing via copy control document reference. Revenue reversal matches original sale amount. Copy control pulls original pricing, not current condition records.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Debit Memo Request?',a:'DR = customer was undercharged. Creates debit memo billing for additional amount. No goods movement. Financial correction upward.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'When does material determination run?',a:'At order entry. Material substitution happens when the order is created. Free goods determination is separate and runs at delivery. Two different processes at different stages.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Return Material Authorization (RMA)?',a:'Authorization number given to customer before returning goods. Returns order with RMA workflow: order created → approval → RMA number → only then return delivery allowed.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What happens if MTO customer cancels after production?',a:'Individual requirement cancelled. Produced stock becomes unrestricted (available to others) or is written off. Decision: sell to another customer, scrap, or charge cancellation fee.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the rebate settlement process?',a:'Rebate accrues during the agreement period. Settlement at period end via VBO1 creates credit memo. Special account keys: ERB (accrual), ERU (settlement). Reverses provision, posts final credit.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are the listing/exclusion condition record transactions?',a:'VB01 (create), VB02 (change), VB03 (display). Similar to pricing and output condition records but for listing/exclusion determination.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can returns be created without reference to original order?',a:'Yes, but risks: wrong pricing (current instead of original), no document flow linkage, manual price override needed. Best practice: always reference original for accurate reversal.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the special GL indicator for down payments?',a:'Identifies the posting as an advance payment in FI. Not regular revenue. Cleared against the final invoice. Separate from normal receivables on the balance sheet.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does material determination interact with pricing?',a:'Material determination substitutes the material FIRST. Then pricing runs on the SUBSTITUTED material. Customer may see different pricing if the substitute has different condition records.',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'Customer returns goods 6 months after purchase. Original order was in a closed fiscal year. How to handle the credit memo?',a:'Credit memo references original invoice. Pricing copies from reference (original amounts). GL posting: current period credit to revenue (reversal). Financial impact on current period P&L, not the closed year. Audit trail: document flow links return to original sale. If pricing type is redetermination (G/H), force it to B (copy) to preserve original amounts.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design a returns process with quality inspection and three possible outcomes: restock, repair, scrap.',a:'Returns Order (RE) → Return Delivery to QI storage location → QM inspection lot → Three paths: (1) Pass: move to unrestricted stock (MIGO 321), credit memo to customer. (2) Repair: move to repair storage, after repair → unrestricted, credit memo. (3) Scrap: move to scrap (MIGO 551), credit memo + scrap cost write-off. Each path has different financial implications.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Material determination needs to substitute Product A with Product B, but only for customers in Region X. Configuration?',a:'Access sequence for material determination includes customer region field. Condition record: Material A + Region X → substitute with Material B. Customers in other regions continue to get Material A. Access sequence is the key — it enables region-specific substitution rules.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Listing/Exclusion with customer hierarchy: parent has exclusion for Material Z, but one subsidiary should be allowed to buy it. Config?',a:'Access sequence checks most-specific first. Maintain a LISTING record at subsidiary level for Material Z (explicitly allowing it). Parent-level exclusion applies to all other subsidiaries. Subsidiary listing overrides parent exclusion because the access sequence hits the more specific record first.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Down payment of ₹5L received. Order is for ₹10L. Customer disputes and wants partial refund of ₹2L. Process?',a:'(1) Down payment clearing cannot be partial in standard. (2) Options: (a) Reverse original down payment, repost for ₹3L (new adjusted amount), refund ₹2L. (b) Create credit memo request for ₹2L against the down payment. (3) Final invoice for ₹10L minus ₹3L remaining down payment = ₹7L due. (4) Ensure FI reconciliation of special GL items.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client has 500 rebate agreements. Year-end settlement takes 3 weeks. Optimization?',a:'(1) Batch settlement via VBO2 with selection criteria (by agreement type, customer group). (2) Partial settlements quarterly (reduce year-end volume). (3) Standardize agreement types (fewer variants = faster processing). (4) Auto-settlement job: background processing with parallel execution. (5) Pre-validation: reconciliation report before settlement run identifies discrepancies early.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Returns without reference to original: customer says "I bought this 2 years ago, no receipt." Business wants to accept. Risk mitigation?',a:'Risks: wrong pricing, no audit trail, potential fraud. Mitigation: (1) Require manager approval (authorization check). (2) Use current lowest price for credit (conservative). (3) Document the exception with reason code. (4) Limit quantity per no-reference return. (5) Reporting: track no-reference returns for pattern analysis (fraud detection).',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Material determination suggests substitute but substitute is also out of stock. What happens?',a:'Depends on config: (1) If substitution is mandatory: order blocked until substitute available. (2) If optional: user can proceed with original material (backorder) or choose another alternative. (3) Chained substitution: first substitute → second substitute (if configured). Most practical: show user both alternatives and let them decide.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design credit memo process with approval workflow for amounts over ₹1L.',a:'(1) Credit memo request (CR) created by sales rep. (2) If value > ₹1L: automatic billing block set. (3) Workflow triggered: notification to regional manager. (4) Manager reviews: approves (removes billing block) or rejects (reason code, notification to sales rep). (5) Below ₹1L: no block, direct billing allowed. (6) Audit: all approvals logged with timestamp and approver.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants to implement a "no questions asked" 30-day return policy. SAP process design?',a:'(1) Returns order type with simplified fields (no reason code required). (2) Copy control from original order/invoice (auto-populate pricing). (3) Return delivery with auto goods receipt to returns storage. (4) Auto credit memo after GR (no approval workflow). (5) Time check: order date + 30 days = returns window. (6) Reporting: returns rate by product/customer for trend analysis. (7) Cost control: returns cost center for tracking.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Rebate accrual shows ₹50L but actual settlement calculates ₹45L. ₹5L discrepancy. Investigation?',a:'(1) Accrual based on invoiced sales × rebate %. Settlement based on actual volumes meeting tier thresholds. (2) If customer didnt reach next tier: accrual over-estimated. (3) Check: were any credit memos/returns excluded from accrual but included in settlement? (4) Exchange rate differences on multi-currency agreements. (5) Adjust accrual provision by ₹5L. Review accrual methodology for accuracy.',type:'troubleshooting'},
]);

const QBExtra = makeQB('Less Focused + ASAP + Cloud', [
{q:'What is rescheduling in SD?',a:'Changes delivery date on open schedule lines. Does NOT change quantity. Can be manual (VA02) or batch job. After PGI: must reverse first.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is FIFO batch strategy?',a:'First In, First Out. Oldest batch (earliest GR date) is consumed first. Standard for most industries. Prevents old stock from sitting indefinitely.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is FEFO batch strategy?',a:'First Expiry, First Out. Batch with earliest expiry date is consumed first. Critical for pharma, food, chemicals. Prevents shipping expired products.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is cross-selling?',a:'Suggesting related products during order entry. Customer buying laptop → suggest bag, mouse, warranty. Uses product proposals and can auto-add items.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are the 5 ASAP phases?',a:'Project Preparation → Business Blueprint → Realization → Final Preparation → Go-Live & Support. Sequential methodology for SAP implementations.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is SAP Activate?',a:'Replaces ASAP for S/4HANA. 6 phases: Discover → Prepare → Explore → Realize → Deploy → Run. Emphasizes Fit-to-Standard and incorporates agile.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Fit-to-Standard?',a:'S/4HANA approach: demonstrate standard SAP processes → identify gaps where standard truly fails → customize only essential gaps. Reduces customization debt.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is RICEFW?',a:'Reports, Interfaces, Conversions, Enhancements, Forms, Workflows. The categories of custom development objects in any SAP implementation.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Clean Core?',a:'S/4HANA philosophy: zero modifications to SAP standard code. All extensions via key user extensibility (in-app) or BTP (side-by-side). Enables smooth quarterly updates.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is SAP BTP?',a:'Business Technology Platform. Side-by-side extension platform for logic that cannot run in Public Cloud core. Connects via APIs. Cloud-native development.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the difference between Public and Private Cloud?',a:'Public: multi-tenant SaaS, quarterly mandatory updates, limited customization. Private: single-tenant, more customization, flexible upgrade schedule.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Can you use SAP GUI in Public Cloud?',a:'No. Public Cloud is Fiori-only. SAP GUI not available. Major change management point for users accustomed to SAP GUI.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is key user extensibility?',a:'In-app extensions without ABAP: custom fields, custom logic (BAdIs), custom CDS views, custom communication scenarios. All upgrade-safe.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is greenfield vs brownfield?',a:'Greenfield: clean start, new config, data migration only. Brownfield: system conversion, keep existing config and data. Selective data transition is a middle ground.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the testing pyramid for SAP projects?',a:'Unit Test (per object) → Integration Test (cross-process) → UAT (business validates) → Performance Test → Regression Test → Cutover Dress Rehearsal.',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'Client has 200 custom ABAP reports in ECC, moving to S/4HANA Public Cloud. Strategy?',a:'Classify: (1) Eliminate obsolete reports (likely 40-50%). (2) Replace with standard Fiori apps or CDS analytical views (30-40%). (3) Rebuild essential ones as BTP apps or Embedded Analytics (10-20%). Target: 80%+ eliminated or replaced. Clean core requires this discipline. Each surviving report needs business justification.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Custom pricing routine (VOFM) wont work in Public Cloud. Alternative architecture?',a:'Standard BAdI for pricing enhancement in Public Cloud. SAP provides specific BAdIs for pricing logic insertion. If insufficient: BTP microservice called via API during pricing execution. Requires architecture redesign but maintains clean core. Document the logic migration for audit trail.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client insists on "pure Agile" for S/4HANA implementation. Your pushback?',a:'Pure Agile doesnt work for ERP: regulatory sign-offs need waterfall gates, data migration cutover is a sequential event, integration testing needs a stable baseline, compliance documentation requires formal review. Recommend hybrid: Agile sprints for config/dev within Activate Realize phase, waterfall governance for Blueprint sign-off, cutover, and go-live decision.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Design cutover plan for S/4HANA go-live. What does a Senior Manager ensure?',a:'Full plan: (1) Data freeze dates and communication. (2) Migration sequence (master data → open documents → transactional data). (3) Fallback/rollback criteria and plan. (4) Go/no-go criteria with specific metrics. (5) Dress rehearsal results reviewed. (6) Hypercare team identified and scheduled. (7) Business communication plan. (8) Support escalation path. Each item has owner, deadline, and sign-off.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Post-go-live: 300 support tickets in first week. Triage approach?',a:'(1) Categorize: blocking (cant process orders/deliveries/billing) vs non-blocking (cosmetic, reporting). (2) Priority: P1 (business stopped), P2 (workaround exists), P3 (enhancement). (3) Blocking issues: war room with functional + technical + business. (4) Daily triage meeting: review all P1/P2, assign owners. (5) Track resolution time. (6) Root cause analysis after stabilization. Most common: master data issues, missing config, user training gaps.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Two-tier ERP: HQ on Private Cloud, subsidiaries on Public Cloud. SD implications?',a:'IC between tiers needs API-based integration (not native IC billing). Master data harmonization critical (BP, material numbers, pricing). Subsidiaries follow Fit-to-Standard (limited customization). HQ has more flexibility. Transfer pricing via integration middleware. Different update cycles (quarterly vs flexible). Higher integration maintenance cost.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client wants embedded analytics to replace 50 custom ABAP reports. Realistic?',a:'Embedded Analytics (CDS views + Fiori analytical apps) can replace 60-70% of typical custom reports. Remaining 30-40%: complex cross-module reports may need BW or BTP. Assessment: catalog all 50 reports, map each to standard analytical content, identify gaps. Realistic timeline: 3-6 months for replacement + validation.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Clean Core assessment on an ECC system with 2000 custom objects. How to prioritize?',a:'(1) Usage analysis: which objects are actually used? (30-40% typically unused). (2) Criticality: which support core business processes? (3) Equivalence: SAP standard replacement available? (4) Complexity: effort to rebuild vs retire. (5) Prioritize: High usage + No standard equivalent = rebuild on BTP. Low usage + Standard exists = retire. Create remediation roadmap with 3 horizons: retire (wave 1), replace (wave 2), rebuild (wave 3).',type:'scenario'},
{q:'BBP sign-off delayed 3 months. Business stakeholders cant agree on TO-BE processes. Impact and action?',a:'Impact: entire project timeline shifts. Realization cannot start without signed BBP. Go-live at risk. Action: (1) Escalate to steering committee and executive sponsor. (2) Propose phased BBP: agree on core 80% (high-volume processes), defer edge cases to Phase 2. (3) Set hard deadline with executive sponsor accountability. (4) Workshop-based conflict resolution with decision log. Never start Realization on unsigned BBP.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Quarterly update in Public Cloud breaks a key user extension. Who is responsible and what is the resolution process?',a:'Customer responsibility — key user extensions are upgrade-safe by framework design, but custom BAdI logic may need adjustment if SAP changes underlying data model. Process: (1) SAP provides early release notes and sandbox environment 4 weeks before update. (2) Customer tests all key user extensions in sandbox. (3) Issues identified → adjust BAdI logic. (4) If breaking change: SAP note/support case for guidance. Prevention: test in sandbox before production update activates.',type:'scenario'},
]);

const QBPublicCloud = makeQB('Public Cloud, CBC & Scope Items', [
{q:'What is Central Business Configuration (CBC) and who typically uses it?',a:'CBC is the central implementation app for S/4HANA Cloud Public Edition. Used primarily by business process experts and consultants (not just technical Basis/ABAP teams) to select and activate Scope Items and complete guided Configuration Activities — without needing SAP GUI or direct IMG access.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is a Scope Item, using a concrete example?',a:'A pre-packaged end-to-end business process shipped by SAP with a unique ID. Example: BD9 = "Sell from Stock" — a standard order-to-cash process bundled with pre-configured settings, a test script, and process flow documentation. Activating BD9 switches on that process for your tenant.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What does a Scope Item ship with?',a:'Four things: (1) pre-configured settings, (2) a test script for validation, (3) process flow documentation (often visualized in SAP Signavio), (4) defined master data requirements needed to run the process.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What are Configuration Activities inside CBC?',a:'Guided, business-friendly steps within an activated scope item — presented as structured questionnaires rather than raw IMG screens. They set values like number ranges, basic org structure elements, or document type selections. Internally they map to the same config tables consultants know from on-premise SPRO.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does CBC differ from SSCUI (Self-Service Configuration UI)?',a:'CBC is the higher-level project cockpit — it sequences which scope items get activated and orchestrates the overall implementation project. SSCUIs are more granular configuration screens; CBC surfaces relevant SSCUIs as part of its guided configuration flow. Think: CBC = cockpit, SSCUI = one of the tools opened from it.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'At what point in a project timeline are Scope Items typically selected?',a:'During the Explore phase of SAP Activate. Fit-to-Standard workshops use scope item process flows (often in Signavio) to validate against business requirements. Confirmed scope items are then activated via CBC — moving from Explore into Realize.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is Fit-to-Standard and how does it relate to Scope Items?',a:'Fit-to-Standard is the SAP Activate methodology step where business stakeholders review standard SAP processes (represented by scope items) against their actual needs, in structured workshops. The goal: confirm what fits as-is, and formally log what doesn\'t (gaps) for the extensibility decision process.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'If a scope item process doesn\'t fully match a customer\'s requirement, can the scope item itself be modified?',a:'No. Scope items are SAP-delivered and cannot be modified at the source (this would break Clean Core and quarterly update compatibility). Instead, the gap is logged and routed through the same extensibility decision tree as any other Public Cloud gap: key user extensibility (in-app) or BTP side-by-side extension.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What role does SAP Signavio play in the Scope Item / CBC process?',a:'Signavio visualizes scope item process flows as diagrams during Fit-to-Standard workshops, helping business stakeholders see the end-to-end standard process (not just a feature list) before confirming or flagging gaps. It\'s the visualization layer that supports the workshop, while CBC is the activation/configuration layer.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Are Configuration Activities a one-time, implementation-only tool?',a:'No. Most Configuration Activities remain accessible after go-live for ongoing changes — e.g., adding new number ranges or org units. They follow standard change control and testing discipline (especially ahead of quarterly updates), same as any config change would.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the risk of skipping or rushing Fit-to-Standard workshops to save time?',a:'Skipping workshops risks activating scope items that don\'t actually match process nuances — meaning gaps surface later, during Realize or testing, rather than early during Explore. Late-discovered gaps are more expensive to fix and put project timelines at higher risk.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'Does SPRO/IMG access still exist in S/4HANA Cloud Public Edition?',a:'Direct SPRO/IMG-style access is largely not exposed to customers in Public Cloud. CBC (and the SSCUIs it surfaces) is the primary configuration interface customers interact with. Full SPRO/IMG access remains standard in Private Cloud and On-Premise editions.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What master data considerations come with activating a new Scope Item?',a:'Each scope item defines master data requirements needed for that process to function (e.g., specific customer, material, or org data setup). Before activation, the implementation team must ensure that required master data exists or is planned as part of the data migration approach.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'How does a Scope Item ID like "BD9" get referenced during a project?',a:'Scope item IDs are used throughout the project: in the CBC activation list, in test scripts referencing that ID, in Signavio process diagrams, and in project documentation/backlogs when logging gaps or configuration decisions tied to that specific process.',type:'conceptual'},
{q:'What is the relationship between a Scope Item\'s test script and go-live readiness?',a:'The test script defines the standard validation steps for that process. Before go-live, testers execute test scripts for all activated scope items to confirm the configured process works end-to-end as SAP intended, catching configuration errors before production use.',type:'conceptual'},
],[
{q:'Client wants to reduce implementation timeline by skipping formal Fit-to-Standard workshops and letting the SD lead unilaterally pick scope items based on experience. Your response?',a:'Push back. Fit-to-Standard workshops exist to validate scope item fit against actual business processes with the people who run them daily — an experienced consultant\'s judgment is a good starting hypothesis, not a substitute for stakeholder validation. Unilateral selection risks gaps surfacing during Realize/testing (expensive) instead of Explore (cheap). Recommend: time-boxed workshops (even compressed to half-days) rather than skipping entirely. The risk in skipping: rework cost typically exceeds the time saved.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'A scope item\'s standard process doesn\'t support a customer\'s specific approval hierarchy for high-value orders. Walk through the resolution path.',a:'(1) Confirm this is a genuine gap during Fit-to-Standard, not a misunderstanding of what the scope item already supports (check Configuration Activities — some approval flexibility may already exist). (2) If genuinely missing: assess key user extensibility first (custom field + BAdI logic may handle simple threshold-based approval). (3) If the approval logic is complex (multi-level, role-based, external system integration): BTP side-by-side workflow extension. (4) Document the decision and cost/timeline impact for the business to approve.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client asks why they can\'t just get IMG/SPRO access "like the old ECC days" for faster configuration. How do you explain this and what\'s the alternative?',a:'Public Cloud deliberately restricts direct SPRO/IMG access — this is what enables the "quarterly update without regression" promise; direct IMG changes could conflict with SAP\'s automated update process. The alternative IS the alternative: CBC + Configuration Activities + SSCUIs cover the configuration scope customers need, presented in a more guided, less error-prone way. Frame it as a trade-off: less raw flexibility, but significantly lower upgrade risk and maintenance burden.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Two scope items appear to overlap for the same business process (e.g., one for standard sales, one for a specific industry variant). How do you decide which to activate?',a:'(1) Compare scope item documentation and Signavio process flows side by side. (2) Run Fit-to-Standard workshop showing both flows to business stakeholders — industry variants often have process steps generic scope items lack (e.g., specific compliance steps). (3) Consider: can you activate the industry variant now to avoid a costly re-implementation later, even if generic seems "good enough" today? (4) Document the decision rationale for future audits.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Post-go-live, business wants to activate an additional scope item that wasn\'t part of the original project. What\'s the process and what risks need managing?',a:'(1) Scope items can typically be activated post-go-live via CBC — it\'s not a one-time-only activation window. (2) Risks: the new scope item may share configuration dependencies with already-active processes (check for conflicts). (3) Master data requirements for the new scope item must be validated against existing data. (4) Test script execution required before enabling for end users. (5) Change control: treat as a mini-project with its own testing and business sign-off, not an ad-hoc toggle.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client\'s existing on-premise system has a heavily customized approval workflow across 15 different document types. They\'re considering Public Cloud. What should the CBC/Scope Item assessment reveal?',a:'This is a red flag for pure Public Cloud fit. Assessment should show: (1) how many of the 15 customizations map to scope items with configurable approval steps (some may be closable via Configuration Activities), (2) how many require BTP workflow extension (higher complexity/cost), (3) whether the sheer volume of custom approval logic suggests Private Cloud (more native customization) is the better-fit deployment model. The CBC/scope item exercise itself becomes the fit assessment — if too many gaps require BTP, that\'s a data point for the deployment model decision, not just an execution detail.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'How would you structure a Fit-to-Standard workshop for the "Sell from Stock" (BD9) scope item with a client who has complex third-party drop-ship requirements?',a:'(1) Walk through BD9\'s standard Signavio process flow first — establish shared understanding of the baseline. (2) Have business walk through their actual drop-ship scenarios against each step, flagging deviations in real time. (3) Common finding: drop-ship may need a different or additional scope item (third-party order processing) rather than being a "gap" in BD9 itself — scope item selection, not just gap-logging, may be the real output. (4) Document: which scope item(s) actually cover their needs, and what remains a genuine gap requiring extensibility.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Client\'s implementation partner wants to activate all "recommended" scope items upfront to save time on future activations. What\'s your concern?',a:'Concern: activating scope items the business hasn\'t validated through Fit-to-Standard creates unused/misconfigured functionality that can (a) confuse end users navigating Fiori launchpad with irrelevant apps, (b) create master data requirements nobody\'s maintaining, (c) complicate future scope item activations if they have configuration dependencies on the pre-activated ones. Recommend: activate only validated scope items; treat "activate everything" as a testing/sandbox exercise, not a production strategy.',type:'scenario'},
{q:'Explain how a Configuration Activity failure or misconfiguration would typically surface, and how you\'d troubleshoot it.',a:'Surfaces via: test script failure during scope item validation, or a Fiori app showing unexpected behavior (missing number range, wrong org assignment). Troubleshooting: (1) Re-open the Configuration Activity in CBC for that scope item — check if a step was skipped or a default value wasn\'t overridden. (2) Cross-check with the underlying config table if you have consultant-level access — Configuration Activities map to the same tables as SPRO objects, so on-premise troubleshooting instincts (checking VOV8-equivalent settings) still apply conceptually. (3) Re-run the test script after correction.',type:'troubleshooting'},
{q:'A prospective client asks: "If CBC handles configuration, do we still need an SAP SD functional consultant for a Public Cloud implementation?" How do you answer?',a:'Yes, and the reasoning matters: CBC makes configuration more guided, but it doesn\'t make the underlying business process decisions for you. A consultant still needs to: interpret Fit-to-Standard workshop findings correctly, know which scope item genuinely fits vs. superficially resembles the need, judge extensibility trade-offs (key user vs. BTP vs. "live with the gap"), and understand SD process fundamentals well enough to configure correctly inside the guided steps. CBC changes the tool, not the expertise required.',type:'conceptual'},
]);

